          "_label": "descriptive note"
        }
      ],
      "content": "the hard brownish-red wood of an East Indian tree, known as Sappan (Cæsalpinia Sappan), from which dyers obtain a red color (OED s.v. Brazil1 I 1) | Wood of the species Caesalpinia sappan, native to Sri Lanka, India, and Malaya. [Source: Getty AAT] | Red dye produced from the hot water extraction of any of several tropical trees of the Caesalpinia genus. Its principal colorant is brasilin, a hydroxyanthraquinone. Used to dye textiles as early as the 12th century in Europe; the color is not lightfast and fades when heated. [Source: Getty AAT]"
    }
  ],
